{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-2x+1,$ show that $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & <{{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking {{field-7}} and {{field-8}} satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist $\\text{positive}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|g(x)|\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\cdot 1\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta \n$$\n\ni.e.,\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le |8{x}^{4}|+|2x|+|1| \\\\  & =8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le 8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1 \\\\  & <8{|\\frac{1}{2}|}^{4}+2|\\frac{1}{2}|+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking $K=3$ and $\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "389bad3a918409e6507ef083a82b655a9c08328ee3cf37bb2dc83d698fc5818e"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "positive"] ["text" "real"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "positive"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-334055" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_