5e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335438" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335438" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the absorption property"] ["text" "the constant multiple property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive  property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335438" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the constant multiple property"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{5})=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5)$  as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  as $x\\to 0$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{4{x}^{2}-x+5}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{x}^{2}-x+5),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-x=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $5$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "389bad3a918409e6507ef083a82b655a9c08328ee3cf37bb2dc83d698fc5818e"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the absorption property"] ["text" "the constant multiple property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the reflexive property"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$"] ["text" "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the absorption property"] ["text" "the constant multiple property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the absorption property"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{L2}"] ["math" "\\text{L3}"] ["math" "\\text{L4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{L4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the absorption property"] ["text" "the constant multiple property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "q-335474" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the constant multiple property"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0034e82136d0f96f12cbd0f2af21b53d51a3833967616675e55a323ea02e503"} {"question" "e-21710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x,$ show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21710.json" "file_sha256" "1dabbf5fd592bf30131b25b56de391308f8a3f44588a6c9f29989d44822e5d9f"} {"question" "e-21710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|g(x)|}\n\\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < |x-a| < \\delta.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|x|}\n \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < |x| < \\delta}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}+4x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{4}|+|{x}^{2}|+|4x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x| < 1,$ then we have ${|x|^2 \\lt |x|}.$ Also, since $|x|<1,$ we have $|x|^4 < |x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x|^4 \\le 3|x|\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|x|^2 \\le |x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x|+4|x| \\\\  & =8|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)| \\leq 8|x|$ whenever $|x| < 1.$ Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and ${\\delta = 1}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21710.json" "file_sha256" "1dabbf5fd592bf30131b25b56de391308f8a3f44588a6c9f29989d44822e5d9f"} {"question" "e-7762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-7762.json" "file_sha256" "6fb75942a794a87fb8e2e0cf8b93fb77eb9cd7d7974c3090c0b137ec18fe21a8"} {"question" "e-7762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-7762.json" "file_sha256" "6fb75942a794a87fb8e2e0cf8b93fb77eb9cd7d7974c3090c0b137ec18fe21a8"} {"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x,$ then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/e52e49265a91cd725c6c5ffb89764144b14f9c7ecd39d8f72c4341337c985492.png) and  the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/04029e3a400b01eb4e063bc8609fc8724b1f9c0b0c0291194e330aead3dee346.png) property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/69fd9b3718fc120ca9106dcb8c7d74154071ba2454634d3b3a2cc59aa64a522c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/819c819776622cbb5c29604cb7c5a6ef3bff8554912b649391b8b4f482aa36b0.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21715.json" "file_sha256" "51bb2f0fc24edb0782618d26e8f7f3de850d8b7992d46a659e8444f21ccbfdb6"} {"question" "e-21715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line  $\\textrm{L3}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\ne^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/example-21715.json" "file_sha256" "51bb2f0fc24edb0782618d26e8f7f3de850d8b7992d46a659e8444f21ccbfdb6"}] "usage" {"e-21710" [] "e-21715" [] "e-7762" [] "q-334693" [] "q-334055" []} "basis" 1503 "retractions" [[17592186157688 :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = 8x^{4} - 2x + 1$, show that $f = O(1)$ as $x → 0$.We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x → a$, and we write\n$f(x) = O(g(x))asx → a$\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $δ$ such that\n\n$f(x) \\le Kg(x)$ {{field-2}}"] [17592186157692 :question/problem "Suppose $f(x) = x + 2e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f = O(x)$ as $x → ∞$\n2. $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$\n3. $f = O(e^{3x})$ as $x → ∞$"] [17592186156982 :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x$, show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$."] [17592186156982 :question/worked-solution "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a$, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & g(x) &} \\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < & x-a\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & x &} \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < \\mid x\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta}\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & &= & -3x^{4} + x^{2} + 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\le\\begin{vmatrix}3x^{4} & + & x^{2} & + & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid x \\mid < 1$, then we have ${\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^2 \\lt & x\\end{vmatrix}}$. Also, since $\\mid x \\mid <1$, we have $\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 < & x\\end{vmatrix}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 \\le 3 & x & \\quad\\text{and}\\quad & x & ^2 \\le & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\mid f(x) \\mid \\mid \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & + & x & + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; = 8 \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq 8 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhenever $\\mid x \\mid < 1$. Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and\n\n$$\n{\\delta = 1}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the definition, and we conclude that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(x)\n$$\n\nas $x\\to 0$."] [17592186157099 :question/problem "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n2. $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n3. $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$"] [17592186157099 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty$. To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x$. Then, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}$.\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$, we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1}; = 1 + lim_(x → ∞)(e^{x} + \\ln x); = 1 + ∞; = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((\\ln x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1/x}; = lim_(x → ∞)x(e^{x} + \\ln x + 1); = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{xe^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x} + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = 0 + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)((\\ln x)^{′})/((e^{x})^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1/x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{xe^{x}}; = 0$.\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186156983 :question/problem "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x$, then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$.\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines $XXXXXXXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$ property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $XXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$."] [17592186156983 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The constant multiple property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The reflexive property.\n$f = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The transitive property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The absorption property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L3}$. From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n$e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$,\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n$\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}}$, we obtain\n$f = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n$f = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1$, it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n$f = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186155294 17592186155295 17592186155297 17592186155298 17592186155299 17592186155300 17592186155341 17592186155342 17592186155343 17592186155344 17592186155345] "immutable_fields" [[17592186155293 :answer-field/id] [17592186155293 :answer-field/key] [17592186155293 :answer-field/type] [17592186155296 :answer-field/id] [17592186155296 :answer-field/key] [17592186155296 :answer-field/type] [17592186155340 :answer-field/correct] [17592186155340 :answer-field/id] [17592186155340 :answer-field/key] [17592186155340 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
